          %h2#Formalization Formalization and Relation to mathematical duality
          %p
            One should beware that the use of the word “duality” in physics is in general different from concepts called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"}> duality
            ” in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
          %p
            For instance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            only simple cases exhibit such obviously “dual” behaviour and in general cases such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U-duality"} U-duality
            really only the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            remains. This more closely resembles the mathematical concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Morita"} Relation to Morita equivalence
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Montonen-Olive+duality"}> Montonen-Olive duality
              \/
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            the equivalence involves some actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            in the mathematical sense, as in replacing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            by its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Langlands+dual+group"} Langlands dual group
          %h3#FormalizationGeneral General formulation
          %p One way to accurately formalize most of what is usually meant by “duality” in physics might instead be the following.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LagrangianData
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LagrangianData
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            data consisting of species of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            and of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangians"} Lagrangians
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            defined on these.
          #LagrangianDataInCaseOfMirrorSymmetry.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For the well-understood case of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
              this would be the usual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} Calabi-Yau manifolds
              regarded as the Lagrangian data for the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 2d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
          %p
            One imagines that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            gives a map from such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum data
            to a moduli stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi QFTs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} QFTs
            of actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi quantization
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi LagrangianData
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi QFTs
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  quantization \;\colon\; LagrangianData \longrightarrow QFTs
                  \\,.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Continuing example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianDataInCaseOfMirrorSymmetry"}
              in the case of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
              this would be the
              = succeed "-construction" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
              to its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+A-%E2%88%9E+category"} Calabi-Yau A-∞ category
              (“of branes”) which defines the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+TQFT"} 2d TQFT
              via the
              %a{:href => "cobordism%20hypothesis#ForNoncompactCobordisms"} noncompact version
              of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-image"} 1-image
            of this map would be the moduli space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian quantum field theories
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi quantization
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi LagrangianData
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi LagrangianQFTs
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi QFTs
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  quantization
                  \\;\colon\;
                  LagrangianData \longrightarrow LagrangianQFTs
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  QFTs
                  \\,.
          %p
            By assumption this is now a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-epimorphism"} 1-epimorphism
            and hence an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
          %p
            The physical concept of duality, such as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi LagrangianData
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_1, L_2 \colon \ast \to LagrangianData
            in the space of Lagrangian data are “dual” to each other, if they become equivalent as quantum field theories after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
          %p
            Mathematically this means that the space of such “dualities” is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} homotopy fiber product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Dualities
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi quantization
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi quantization
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianQFTs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Dualities
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  LagrangianData &amp;&amp; \swArrow_{\simeq} &amp;&amp; LagrangianData
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{quantization}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{quantization}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; LagrangianQFTs
                  }
          %p
            By definition, an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Dualities
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dualities
            is two Lagrangians and a choice of equivalence of their associated quantum field theories:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi quantization
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi quantization
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  quantization(L_1) \simeq quantization(L_2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This construction is the first step in associating the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            which is induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            “quantization” via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p
            It continues in the way that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+covers"} Cech covers
            do (whence one speaks of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+nerve"} Cech nerve
            construction of the quantization map
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LagrangianData
                    %mo →
                    %mi LagrangianQFTs
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LagrangianData \to LagrangianQFTs
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dualities
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dualities
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi DualitiesOfdualities
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                          %munder
                            %mo ×
                            %mi LagrangianQFT
                          %mi LagrangianData
                          %munder
                            %mo ×
                            %mi LagrangianQFT
                          %mi LagrangianDat
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Dualities
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                          %munder
                            %mo ×
                            %mi LagrangianQFT
                          %mi LagrangianData
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \vdots
                  \\\
                  DualitiesOfdualities &amp;\simeq&amp; LagrangianData \underset{LagrangianQFT}{\times} LagrangianData \underset{LagrangianQFT}{\times} LagrangianDat
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow \downarrow \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Dualities &amp;\simeq&amp; LagrangianData \underset{LagrangianQFT}{\times} LagrangianData
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; LagrangianData
                  }
          %p
            It continues this way through all
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LagrangianData
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LagrangianData
            and as moduli stack of 1-morphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Dualities
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Dualities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            realization is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LagrangianQFTs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LagrangianQFTs
            and so the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/augmented+simplicial+set"} augmented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            looks as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi DualitiesOfDualities
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Dualities
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi quantization
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianQFTs
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\vdots
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow\downarrow\downarrow\downarrow
                  \\\
                  DualitiesOfDualities
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow\downarrow\downarrow
                  \\\
                  Dualities
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow \downarrow
                  \\\
                  LagrangianData
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{quantization}}
                  \\\
                  LagrangianQFTs
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Such towers are to be thought of as the incarnation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
            as we pass to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            is just the first stage of such a tower
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Dualities
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Lagrangians
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Dualities
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Lagrangians
                  }
          %p
            The conditions on an equivalence relation – reflexivity, transitivity, symmetry – may be read as those on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object"} groupoid object
            – identity, composition, inverses. So now in homotopy logic this is boosted to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} groupoid object in an (∞,1)-category
            by relaxing all three to hold only up to higher coherent homotopies.
          %p The bottom-most arrow
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianData
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi quantization
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi LagrangianQFTs
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  LagrangianData
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{quantization}}
                  \\\
                  LagrangianQFTs
                  }
          %p
            is the quotient projection of the equivalence relation. In 1-logic this would be its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cokernel"} cokernel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
            over the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
            diagram.
          %p So the perspective of the full diagram gives the usual way of speaking in QFT also a reverse:
          %p instead of saying
          %p a) that two Lagrangians are dual if there is an equivalence between the QFTs which they induce under quantization,
          %p we may turn this around and say that therefore
          %p
            b) quantization is the result of forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
            of the space of Lagrangian data by these duality relations.
          %p
            It is one of the clauses of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud+theorem"} Giraud theorem
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos+theory"} (∞,1)-topos theory
            that these two perspectives are equivalent.
          %h3#FormalizationExamples Examples
          %p
            For a concrete example of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FormalizationGeneral"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theories"} Chern-Simons theories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rozansky-Witten+theories"} Rozansky-Witten theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
            they are very different, but after
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian data
            maps to the same abstract vector space of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+knot+invariants"} Vassiliev knot invariants
          %p
            Concretely, in both cases the map from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian data
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            is given by the assignment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems"} weight systems
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+diagrams"} Jacobi diagrams
          %p
            1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+systems"} Lie algebra weight systems
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AssignLieAlgebraWeightSystems.jpg", :width => "440"}/
          %p
            2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rozansky-Witten+weight+systems"} Rozansky-Witten weight systems
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rozansky-Witten+theory"} Rozansky-Witten theory
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AssignRozanskyWittenWeightSystems.jpg", :width => "440"}/
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop"} Wilson loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            are the evaluation of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems"} weight systems
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+Vassiliev+invariant"} universal Vassiliev invariant
          %p
            1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop+observables"} Wilson loop observables
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TheGrandStoryOfVassilievKnotInvariantsIII.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %p
            2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rozansky-Witten+theory"} Rozansky-Witten theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop+observables"} Wilson loop observables
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TheGrandStoryOfRozanskyWittenKnotInvariantsIII.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %p Hence in total we have the following quantization map from Lagrangian data to observables:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AssignCSAndRWObservables.jpg", :width => "440"}/
          %p
            To the extent that all observables of these theories, on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-sphere"} 3-sphere
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+loop+observables"} Wilson loop observables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
            of this quantization map with itself:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/DualitiesOfCSAndRWTheories.jpg", :width => "400"}/
          %h3#Morita Relation to Morita equivalence
          %p
            According to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Schwarz04"} Schwarz 04
          %blockquote
            %p
              I am convinced that the mathematical notion of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebras"} associative algebras
              and its generalization for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+algebra"} differential associative algebras
              should be regarded as the mathematical foundation of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} dualities in string/M-theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Schwarz"} Schwarz
            showed that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactifications"} KK-compactifications
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+tori"} noncommutative tori
            are physically equivalent (
            %a{:href => "#Schwarz98"}> Schwarz98
            ). This work is followed up in relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            in
            %a{:href => "#Pioline99"} Pioline99
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CNS11"} CNS11
          %p
            %a{:href => "#BMRS08"} BMRS08
            discusses an axiomatic definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+T-duality"} topological T-duality
            generalizing and refining T-duality between noncommutative spaces in terms of Morita equivalence to a special type of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-equivalence
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#Okada09"} Okada09
            a variant of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
            is shown to be a form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+Morita+equivalence"} derived Morita equivalence
          %p
            Morita theoretic ideas are also involved in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+homology"} factorization homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+complex"} blob complex
            and premodular TQFTS, see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MW10"} MW10
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BZBJ15"} BZBJ15
            %a{:href => "#Scheimbauer"} Scheimbauer
            for the Morita
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty, n)
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
          %h2#duality_after_compactification Duality after compactification
          %p
            Informal comments on a possible general picture of duality in physics arising from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "duality+in+string+theory#BrennanCartaVafa17"} Brennan, Carta &amp; Vafa 17, p. 7
          %blockquote
            %p
              Since we have seen that the full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theories"} string theories
              are all interrelated by a sequence of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} dualities
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactifications
              are also related by dualities. As it turns out, these relations are so abundant that we can make the following observation:
            %p
              %strong> “Conjecture”
              \: Whenever the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+of+supersymmetries"} number of preserved supercharges
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+fermion"} chiralities
              of two different
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+vacuum"} compactifications of string theory
              match, there are choices of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactification geometries
              such that they are dual descriptions of the same physical theory.
            %p
              Surprisingly, we are aware of no known counter examples. In this sense, dualities in lower dimensional theories are not hard to find, but rather are hard to prevent! One rationale for the existence of dualities is as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergio+Cecotti"} Sergio Cecotti
              puts it, “the scarcity of rich structures”. In particular the very existence of quantum systems of gravity is hard to arrange and if we succeed to get more than one theory with a given symmetry, there is a good chance we have landed on the same theory.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parent+action+functional"} parent action functional
          %p
            There is also a duality in the
            %em description
            of physics:
